WEBVTT

00:00:00.039 --> 00:00:01.920
In a few seconds, could you explain

00:00:01.960 --> 00:00:03.920
that to people who might go, "What the

00:00:03.960 --> 00:00:06.560
hell do you mean conflict, confidence versus

00:00:06.600 --> 00:00:08.719
desire to win?" It's hard to describe

00:00:08.759 --> 00:00:10.080
in a few seconds, but I'll try

00:00:10.120 --> 00:00:11.520
my best. So, we know that there are

00:00:11.560 --> 00:00:12.400
a couple of factors.

00:00:12.440 --> 00:00:13.920
There are many factors, but there's two that

00:00:13.960 --> 00:00:15.280
we kind of choose to focus on because

00:00:15.320 --> 00:00:16.800
they're easy to get hold of.

00:00:16.840 --> 00:00:18.640
How confident am I in general about a

00:00:18.680 --> 00:00:19.999
conflict? So, we'll know those people.

00:00:20.039 --> 00:00:21.440
It's like water off a duck's back.

00:00:21.480 --> 00:00:22.800
Yeah. I hit them with it.

00:00:22.840 --> 00:00:23.440
I'm all right.

00:00:23.480 --> 00:00:25.120
I can kind of tolerate that discomfort.

00:00:25.160 --> 00:00:26.560
So, you would say you have high conflict

00:00:26.600 --> 00:00:28.560
confidence. There are others of us who would

00:00:28.600 --> 00:00:31.640
do almost anything to avoid even a slightly

00:00:31.880 --> 00:00:33.680
edgy conversation. So then you would we

00:00:33.720 --> 00:00:35.560
would say we'd have low confidence.

00:00:35.799 --> 00:00:37.960
But it actually that's not the only story

00:00:38.039 --> 00:00:39.920
because if you take our desire to win,

00:00:39.960 --> 00:00:42.560
our desire to either get it right or

00:00:42.600 --> 00:00:44.960
be right. And often we do have a

00:00:45.000 --> 00:00:47.360
strong desire to be right because it's very

00:00:47.400 --> 00:00:49.520
attached to our kind of sense of self.

00:00:49.560 --> 00:00:51.360
If we have a high desire to get

00:00:51.400 --> 00:00:53.600
it right, like solve the problem or be

00:00:53.640 --> 00:00:56.800
right, we can often overcome a low set

00:00:56.840 --> 00:00:58.800
of conflict confidence to go in and have

00:00:58.840 --> 00:01:00.719
a hard conversation, but we might do it

00:01:00.759 --> 00:01:02.800
in a way that we're overcorrecting.

00:01:02.840 --> 00:01:04.160
So, we're kind of getting into that

00:01:04.200 --> 00:01:07.280
inflexible space. So, we will come back

00:01:07.320 --> 00:01:08.040
maybe

00:01:09.960 --> 00:01:12.239
I mean it's I have to do the

00:01:12.279 --> 00:01:14.080
online assessment because I was going wonder

00:01:14.120 --> 00:01:17.920
what is my own default conflict style.

00:01:18.279 --> 00:01:20.880
and you know, I was going, okay,

00:01:20.920 --> 00:01:22.560
I can be I think back to my

00:01:22.600 --> 00:01:24.320
upbringing, there was a little bit of

00:01:24.360 --> 00:01:26.320
ignoring as children if there was a bit

00:01:26.360 --> 00:01:28.320
of conflict. We got a bit of that.

00:01:28.360 --> 00:01:30.160
And I think some I've tried to move

00:01:30.200 --> 00:01:31.920
past that. I do in the workplace.

00:01:31.960 --> 00:01:33.040
It never happens.

00:01:33.080 --> 00:01:34.400
But sometimes I go, "Oh, you know what?

00:01:34.440 --> 00:01:35.759
That person's really annoyed me now.

00:01:35.799 --> 00:01:37.999
I'm just going to take a step back,

00:01:38.039 --> 00:01:39.160
set a boundary,

00:01:39.640 --> 00:01:41.759
blah blah blah." But it's not always the

00:01:41.799 --> 00:01:43.040
right thing to do.

00:01:43.080 --> 00:01:44.400
The other thing I loved in the book

00:01:44.440 --> 00:01:46.800
which we touched on is this enforced harmony

00:01:46.840 --> 00:01:48.040
within teams

00:01:48.360 --> 00:01:50.640
and you know we see that all the

00:01:50.680 --> 00:01:53.600
time teams like oh we've got a nice

00:01:53.640 --> 00:01:54.360
culture

00:01:54.759 --> 00:01:55.880
but actually

00:01:56.519 --> 00:01:59.120
you know you kind of want some healthy

00:01:59.160 --> 00:01:59.880
disagreement.

00:02:00.200 --> 00:02:02.719
Is there any kind of strategies you say

00:02:02.759 --> 00:02:04.840
that can help people

00:02:05.400 --> 00:02:08.320
have or teams have let's call it healthy

00:02:08.360 --> 00:02:09.080
disagreement?

00:02:10.120 --> 00:02:11.600
I mean, I think one of the biggest

00:02:11.640 --> 00:02:14.719
unlocks when we do the workshop with even

00:02:14.759 --> 00:02:17.200
very senior people is making the mindset

00:02:17.240 --> 00:02:19.040
shift between the relational conflict.

00:02:19.080 --> 00:02:20.719
So, it's like a game of singles tennis.

00:02:20.759 --> 00:02:22.479
Yeah. So, it's like I'm right, you're wrong,

00:02:22.519 --> 00:02:24.680
I win, you lose, and we're scoring points

00:02:24.759 --> 00:02:26.920
to getting into task conflict,

00:02:27.240 --> 00:02:29.360
which is like a game of doubles tennis,

00:02:29.400 --> 00:02:31.360
which is you and me against the problem.

00:02:31.400 --> 00:02:33.200
And even taking that mindset of saying,

00:02:33.240 --> 00:02:34.719
"Okay, I notice that I'm in this game

00:02:34.759 --> 00:02:35.960
of singles tennis.

00:02:36.039 --> 00:02:37.520
How do I get this person to join

00:02:37.560 --> 00:02:38.880
me on the other side of the net

00:02:38.920 --> 00:02:41.120
where we're trying to say solve the same

00:02:41.160 --> 00:02:43.600
problem together? And in the book, there are

00:02:43.640 --> 00:02:45.280
tons of techniques about how to do this

00:02:45.320 --> 00:02:47.680
practically. But even we sometimes see it on

00:02:47.720 --> 00:02:49.520
the faces of the workshop participants and

00:02:49.560 --> 00:02:51.360
they go, I have never seen it like

00:02:51.400 --> 00:02:53.040
that before and now I've seen it.

00:02:53.080 --> 00:02:55.520
I can't unsee it and this is unlocking

00:02:55.560 --> 00:02:58.040
for me how I'm going to move somebody

00:02:58.360 --> 00:02:59.759
to the side of the net that I'm

00:02:59.799 --> 00:03:01.360
standing on. So I think that's one of the

00:03:01.400 --> 00:03:04.239
big things. I think one very specific and

00:03:04.279 --> 00:03:06.239
practical thing which builds on some of the

00:03:06.279 --> 00:03:08.400
conversation we've had is we use a phrase

00:03:08.440 --> 00:03:10.320
in the book that has also made its

00:03:10.360 --> 00:03:12.160
way onto a tea towel out of pure

00:03:12.200 --> 00:03:14.239
demand. I know some of these details.

00:03:14.279 --> 00:03:14.800
I know. I know.

00:03:14.840 --> 00:03:15.999
I think they're actually arriving at my

00:03:16.039 --> 00:03:17.360
house a bit of the Christmas order in

00:03:17.400 --> 00:03:17.999
a few hours.

00:03:18.039 --> 00:03:18.880
So, don't worry.

00:03:18.920 --> 00:03:20.640
we'll pop your name on one.

00:03:20.680 --> 00:03:22.920
It's get curious not furious.

00:03:22.999 --> 00:03:24.719
And I think what's interesting about this,

00:03:24.759 --> 00:03:26.080
which is, you know, how do you stay

00:03:26.120 --> 00:03:29.040
in that curiosity space is that that applies

00:03:29.080 --> 00:03:31.360
whether you're avoidant or whether you tend

00:03:31.400 --> 00:03:33.120
to kind of go nought to 100.

00:03:33.160 --> 00:03:34.640
Because if you're someone that goes n to

00:03:34.680 --> 00:03:37.040
100, we're saying notice that you've got

00:03:37.080 --> 00:03:38.440
that you're getting furious.

00:03:38.759 --> 00:03:40.280
Just suspend judgement.

00:03:40.680 --> 00:03:41.999
You know, if you need to, if

00:03:42.039 --> 00:03:43.280
that means you need to walk away from

00:03:43.320 --> 00:03:44.959
this conversation until you're in a better

00:03:44.999 --> 00:03:46.400
place, fine, do that.

00:03:46.440 --> 00:03:48.959
Prioritise that. Or can you just go, I've

00:03:48.999 --> 00:03:50.719
noticed I'm getting furious to kind of start

00:03:50.759 --> 00:03:52.560
to own the feelings and sort of get

00:03:52.600 --> 00:03:54.959
out of that amygdala hijack in that moment

00:03:54.999 --> 00:03:57.520
and then go, what's my prepared question

00:03:57.560 --> 00:04:00.320
that will just at least announce that I'm

00:04:00.360 --> 00:04:02.160
curious and get into this thing.

00:04:02.200 --> 00:04:04.640
So, but also if we're avoidant and we're

00:04:04.680 --> 00:04:05.400
thinking,

00:04:05.880 --> 00:04:07.600
what will they say if I share my

00:04:07.640 --> 00:04:09.320
true thoughts right now?

00:04:09.560 --> 00:04:11.560
What if you were just getting curious?

00:04:11.720 --> 00:04:14.239
Yeah, in a way, you know, so

00:04:14.279 --> 00:04:16.360
it it's not it's get curious,

00:04:17.160 --> 00:04:19.360
don't worry about them getting furious kind

00:04:19.400 --> 00:04:21.040
of thing, if that makes sense, you know,

00:04:21.080 --> 00:04:22.959
because so there's still I think I think

00:04:22.999 --> 00:04:25.200
it's a useful phrase whichever end of the

00:04:25.240 --> 00:04:25.720
scale you're operating on.
